            "text": "{\n  \"relation\": \"REFINED\",\n  \"consensus_summary\": \"A polyvalent symbol whose meaning depends on context, most prominently representing Jesus Christ in his royal, conquering power, but also commonly symbolizing Satan as a devouring predator, hostile human empires, divine judgment, or general courage.\",\n  \"book_summary\": \"A royal and devouring ruler or empire, encompassing wicked political powers (like Assyria and Babylon) as the predatory manifestation, and Christ (the Lion of Judah) as the rightful holder of this authority.\",\n  \"consensus_core_identification\": \"A context-dependent symbol of power, primarily denoting Christ's kingship but also Satanic threat, hostile nations, or God's wrath.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"A royal and devouring ruler or empire.\",\n  \"core_relation\": \"BOOK_NARROWS\",\n  \"extension_relation\": \"BOOK_SUBSET\",\n  \"boundary_disagreement\": \"The book restricts the symbol strictly to rulers, princes, and empires, unifying the positive and negative uses under a single category of rulership. In doing so, it excludes the consensus's non-ruler applications, such as the devil (1 Peter 5:8), God's direct prophetic wrath (Hosea/Amos), and generic courage (Proverbs 30).\",\n  \"material_contradiction\": \"There is no material contradiction regarding the cases the book includes. The book accepts both the Christological and the wicked-empire readings, but systematizes them into a single, cohesive concept of rulership rather than treating the symbol as disjointedly polyvalent.\",\n  \"rationale\": \"The consensus views the lion as a polyvalent symbol with several separate meanings (Christ, Satan, empires, God, courage). The book refines this by identifying a single, underlying referent—rulers or empires—that explains both the predatory ('devouring') and legitimate ('royal') usages, thereby omitting the broader, non-political usages.\",\n  \"citation_support\": \"STRONG\",\n  \"strongest_objection\": \"By narrowing the symbol to 'rulers and empires', the book omits one of the most famous and widely taught New Testament usages: the devil prowling like a roaring lion (1 Peter 5:8). While the devil could be construed as a 'wicked ruler', the lack of explicit inclusion leaves a major consensus element unaddressed.\",\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
